	"notes": "This blog puts two things on the page at once above 950px: a column of article summaries on the left and a narrower sidebar of categories and popular posts on the right, both a fixed number of pixels wide. Because neither column stretches, a giant ultrawide monitor does not get a wider layout — it just gets more empty space on the left and right, so the page always looks the same width. Below about 950px, the two columns become one: the sidebar's content does not vanish, it just moves down to the bottom of the page, after the last article. The scout guessed this page would be a plain single centred column (`1-centered`), but the scan shows a real two-track CSS grid the whole time — a case where the picture alone (a column with a wide right margin) could be read either way, and only the CSS settles it.",
